\f1\fs22 Although Eloise assessed the task Sarah assigned Kathy as tangential, mystifying and probably nothing more than indulgence, she was prepared to let Sarah have her head. Compared with the kind of random, capricious things Alexander begged for, the task Sarah set Kathy was adjacent enough to the bell curve of statistically normal. Eloise could allow it. While she agreed to let Kathy roam free without a security detail, she still deployed every surveillance trick in the book of espionage and contract bridge to watch Kathy like an arms-dealing hawk.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>While Sarah was ensconced in Eloise\'92s office receiving her surprise supplicant, Eloise retreated to a more private space on the floor and worked on normal daily matters while monitoring Kathy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy took a company hover-copt and flew into the Ghetto. That raised Eloise\'92s eyebrow.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy met with the High Elvis, who greeted her as Aunt Kathy. Eloise\'92s lips pursed, thin with contemplation. Had the Elvi somehow learned about Eloise naming the Three Sisters? Or was it coincidence? Perhaps there was some reverence for Kathy for the way she stood by the Holy Momma.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis called for a squad of Tulsa Macleans, who were experts in martial arts. They were quickly assembled, wearing combat jumpsuits, ponchos and berets. They carried guitars, extra strings, and plectrums. Their hair was immaculate, their sunglasses dazzling, their smiles irrepressible and their rifles\'97how the tax did they get those semi-automatics? Eloise scrabbled for her tablet and started tracking down someone who could give her a full report inside five minutes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy, the High Elvis, other dignitaries, and the combat unit took a cavalcade of Cadillacs and Lincoln Continentals with makeshift blue police lights affixed to their roofs to the Quarter\'92s Travelator station.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise put aside daily matters and cancelled all meetings while she gave her complete attention to Kathy and her entourage.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s late,\'94 Kathy said as she stood with the High Elvis on the platform.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis raised an eyebrow yet somehow remained cordial. \'93Travelator Uncertainty, Aunt Kathy.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, Sarah\'97\'94 Kathy began and stopped short as the full detachment of gun-toting Tulsas, who had spread up and down the platform and were alert for any incursion, snapped indignant stares at her. \'93I mean, the Holy Momma instructed we must collect\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, Aunt Kathy.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>So they waited. The key was to give up any expectation of when the Travelator would arrive.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So it arrives when we stop caring if it arrives?\'94 Kathy asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I wouldn\'92t put that expectation on it, Aunt Kathy,\'94 the High Elvis counselled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy tried not to care. She put another patch on her other arm, just to make sure. And they waited. After an hour, Kathy thought she saw a couple of the Tulsa Macleans getting restless. \'93Hey, you,\'94 she called down the platform. \'93Stop caring.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>One Tulsa had propped his semi-automatic against the bench and was pretending to tune his guitar while keeping one boot on the stock, as though maintaining contact with a weapon by footwear alone constituted operational readiness. Another had lowered himself into a sitting position so gradually that, from a distance, he appeared to still be standing, only shorter. His poncho pooled around him on the platform like a deflating tent. A third stood with his rifle held across his body at what might charitably be called parade rest, if parades could rest, and periodically adjusted his sunglasses with the muzzle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi realised this was a perfect scene for a song. So they tuned their guitars and sang one. Kathy didn\'92t recognise the tune; there were so many Elvi songs these days, it was hard to keep up with them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93This is insane,\'94 Kathy announced to nobody who was listening. \'93I\'92m standing on a platform with an army of Elvis clones carrying semi-automatic rifles and guitars, waiting for a train that turns up when it feels like it, and I\'92m the one who looks out of place because I haven\'92t got a poncho.\'94 She checked the patch on her arm. The fog was starting to creep at the edges. She\'92d need another one soon, and she hadn\'92t packed for a siege.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The morning wore on. Kathy noticed the High Elvis shifting his weight from foot to foot, his cordial composure developing hairline cracks. \'93I should have ordered fried peanut butter and banana sandwiches,\'94 he said, with the air of a man confessing a tactical failure. As lunchtime came and went, the Tulsas lost their paramilitary air of vigilance and began to slouch, their rifle muzzles pointing at their toes. Some sat on the platform.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, tax it,\'94 Kathy said. Her second arm was running out of fresh patch real estate, and the fog wasn\'92t waiting politely at the edges any more. \'93I\'92m calling it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They started trudging off the platform like children who had played well past their bedtime. Kathy and the High Elvis waited until the last to leave.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That\'92s when the Travelator appeared. Kathy blinked. Had the Travelator rolled out of the tunnel and down the line to the platform, or had it materialised in place out of thin nowhere? Kathy frowned. She could not recall.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Which carriage is theirs?\'94 she asked the High Elvis. She needn\'92t have worried. From the carriage directly before her emerged three bearded wizened men wearing florid suits pulling suitcases on wheels. They walked up to Kathy and greeted her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We\'92ve been expecting you,\'94 said Kleoboulos Windus, Principal Prophecy Facilitator of Seven Sages Corp. \'93If it isn\'92t too much trouble, might we proceed? The prophecy has a schedule, even if the Travelator doesn\'92t.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On the sixty-third floor of a building that existed for the sole purpose of not appearing to exist, Eloise watched the three wizened figures step onto the platform on her surveillance feed, and reached for a fresh cup of tea.
